From 2675211b77c2018cfdb4cf0fab143219e32454ea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DeltaVector1 <DeltaVector1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 14:08:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 32/32] Set max_inflight_rollouts to 192; the two knobs
 interact

192 in flight measured 14.1/min on 24 tunnels and 23.2/min on 48, while 128 in
flight measured the same 14/min on both. Tunnel count does nothing until the
in-flight count is high enough to need it, which is why doubling the tunnels
alone read as a null result. Engine concurrency went 26 -> 46 and combined
token throughput about 899 -> 1126 tok/s.

Also record a methodology fault. Windowed rate measurements are unreliable
here: two 9-minute windows at the same setting gave 23.18 and 8.10 rollouts a
minute. A window lands at a random point in a 44 to 84 minute batch cycle, and
the rate collapses at the tail when the orchestrator stops opening work. Token
throughput has the same contamination: 1369 then 883 tok/s at one setting.

Use the orchestrator's own per-batch cycle time instead. It averages the whole
cycle including the tail, and it is the number that sets the step time:

  step 6  137.4 min  78.3% error   (256 in flight, no compression)
  step 7  118.3 min  18.9% error   (128, compression landed mid-batch)
  step 8   82.6 min   5.1% error   (128, 24 tunnels, bounded timeouts)

Step 9 is the first batch at 192 in flight on 48 tunnels.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_017wiESxhziDrnsLnG9aizTd
---
 configs/china/orchestrator.x24.toml | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/configs/china/orchestrator.x24.toml b/configs/china/orchestrator.x24.toml
index 7760116..d87154a 100644
--- a/configs/china/orchestrator.x24.toml
+++ b/configs/china/orchestrator.x24.toml
@@ -3,7 +3,7 @@ max_steps = 200
 seq_len = 16384
 batch_size = 1024
 group_size = 16
-max_inflight_rollouts = 128
+max_inflight_rollouts = 192
 # 4, not 2: batches go out over ZMQ fire-and-forget, so a trainer restart
 # loses whatever was in flight. With a tight cap the orchestrator then holds
 # for a policy the restarted trainer cannot publish and both sides wait
